 ],
 "literal": "Desire, desire, they say. Desire / is not just an affliction or a sickness. Becoming subtle, / it has no sudden surging or subsiding. Like the rut / of an elephant that chewed leafy shoots, / it also has a period of waiting, if only it finds those who can perceive it.",
 "translation": "People talk of desire, of desire. But desire is not merely a tormenting spirit or a disease. It is subtle; it does not suddenly surge or subside. Like the rut of an elephant that has chewed leafy shoots, it has a period of quiet waiting, if only it finds those who can truly perceive it.",
 "modern": "காமம் காமம் என்று எல்லாரும் சொல்கிறார்கள். காமம் என்பது வெறும் வருத்தமோ அல்லது நோயோ அல்ல. அது நுட்பமாகி, திடீரென மிகுவதோ அல்லது தணிவதோ இல்லாதது. தழைகளைத் தின்ற யானையின் மதநீர் போல, அதைப் புரிந்து கொள்பவர்களைப் பெற்றால், அது வெளிப்படாமல் அடங்கி இருக்கும் காலத்தையும் உடையது.",
 "explanation": "The hero speaks to himself about the deep nature of his love for the heroine. People often speak of desire as if it were a simple affliction or a disease that comes and goes. But the hero explains that true love is subtle and deep; it does not have sudden, erratic surges or quick cures. He compares his love to the rut of an elephant that has fed on leafy shoots. Just as the elephant's rut remains hidden and builds up slowly within, only showing itself to those who know how to look, his love has a period of quiet waiting when there is someone who can truly understand and perceive it. The imagery of the mountain elephant feeding on forest leaves belongs to the Kurinji landscape, symbolizing the deep, enduring, and internal nature of their union.",
